  :adr/body
  "## What was asked

Identify JVM dependence repo-wide and resolve it with ClojureScript or Kotoba only.

## What the numbers actually were

Every headline figure moved once the code behind it was read.

| claim | first measured | after reading the code |
|---|---|---|
| repos where the JVM is the RUNTIME | 771 | **233** |
| third-party JVM libs to replace | 34 (cheshire) + 5 others | **0 remain** |
| libraries that had to be built | 6 | **0** (all existed) |
| files blocking ~184 repos | 3 | **0** (deliberate boundary) |

### jvm-source 771 -> 233

514 of the 768 repos shipping production `.clj` have NOTHING but files carrying
a `-main`. 472 are a single `render_html.clj` invoked by their own deps.edn as
`:render-html {:main-opts [\"-m\" ...]}` to regenerate a docs page. Three checks,
not one: the invocation is an alias not a require; only 5 places in the whole
workspace require a `*.render-html` namespace; of the 514, exactly 3 have their
entrypoint required by other code.

ADR-2607198300 already said a build-time JVM is not the gap. The detector was
contradicting the ADR it exists to enforce. Fixed in the detector (b697795), not
in prose, because a number nobody can act on is worse than no number.

### The libraries all existed

`kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang`'s foundational-stdlib ADR names 16 repos -- `coll`
`spec` `json` `wit` `async` `time` `fs` `http` `io` `test` `fmt` `lsp`
`scheduler` `store` `lint` `text` -- all registered, all M6. Asked to build libs
for `slurp`/`spit`/`format`/`future`, the answer for five of six was that the
lib was already there.

`slurp`/`spit`/`future` are ABSENT BY DESIGN, not missing. `fs` ships \"pure path
manipulation plus an IFilesystem protocol the host injects\"; `async` ships
\"bounded channels as pure state machines\". Ambient authority is the thing this
stdlib exists to remove. Implementing portable `slurp` would have contradicted
the architecture it was landing in.

## What was actually missing, and built

Two host implementations. Both had a protocol, a contract and a reference
provider already; what was absent was the binding to a real resource.

- `provider.http-ingress-transport` (kotoba-lang/provider ed0db86) -- `node:http`
  behind the ingress capability. `amu/test/nbb/http-ingress-provider.cljs` looked
  like this existed; it never opens a socket.
- `kotoba.lang.fs-host` (kotoba-lang/fs 607caf1) -- a real filesystem behind
  `IFilesystem`, root-confined, refusals lifted verbatim from
  `provider.scoped-fs`. `fs.cljc` untouched: 0 host references, 0 diff.

And one real gap inside an existing lib: `text/format` handled bare `%s %d %x
%f` and emitted `\"%.\"` plus literal text for anything else. Of the 5,968
specifiers in use, 944 (16%) carry width or precision -- `%.1f` 176, `%02x` 133,
`%064x` 17. `%064x` is how a 32-byte hash is rendered. Now a full printf
grammar, varargs like clojure.core/format, verified DIFFERENTIALLY against
clojure.core/format over 37 cases (ab981a4).

## Third-party JVM libs: 34 -> 0

cheshire was 34 repos. The whole surface in use was two functions. A rename
would have SILENTLY broken 37 call sites -- `(parse-string s true)` keywordizes,
`json.core/decode` returns string keys, and the difference compiles, runs, and
returns nil from every keyword lookup. So `json.compat` pins three behaviours
(keywordize, nil->nil, `{:pretty true}`) and migration became a require line.

The last repo needed more: kotoba-captcha caught Jackson's class to answer 400
before a general ExceptionInfo answered 500. Clause ORDER carried that meaning.
Fixed in the library -- `:type :json/parse-error` -- because a JVM class name
cannot cross to cljs. That path had NO TEST while the class guarded it; it has
one now, and disabling the branch gives `(not (= 400 500))`.

Four other deps were declared and never required: transit-clj x2, next.jdbc,
jline. Removed.

## The failure mode this session kept producing

A check that could not answer returned the same value as one that answered no.
Seven times, in my own tools:

1. `git grep` non-zero exit read as \"not found\" -- the fsmonitor daemon was
   dying, so ERRORS were recorded as DEAD dependencies
2. `git grep -q -- \"$pat\"` -- `--` makes the pattern a PATHSPEC, so nothing was
   searched and everything came back USED
3. `grep -r` with an explicit FILE LIST returns nothing on macOS; 27 repos read
   as \"cheshire unused\"
4. `xargs -I{}` splits on tabs, so every `gh api` got a truncated argument and
   failed -- scored as \"file absent\", nearly reporting 189/189 bb.edn GONE when
   the truth was 189/189 PRESENT
5. a `perl -0pi` break whose regex did not match, leaving the file untouched and
   reporting green as a successful demonstration
6. a `python` replace that matched inside an existing token, turning `cstr/` into
   `ccstr/`
7. `west-pin-put-batch` took the first `- name: X`, which for a repo sharing an
   org's name is the REMOTES block -- the pin was silently dropped (fail-closed,
   so no wrong pin was written, but it could never advance)

Every one produced a plausible number. The tell each time was uniformity: all
DEAD, all USED, all GONE. A real population is not unanimous.

## What is left

- **233 repos** where the JVM is genuinely the runtime. Largest cluster is 32
  `cacao.clj`, which CLAUDE.md explicitly protects from retroactive rewriting.
- **1,415 slurp/spit call sites**, but 1,200 are in `src/` of which most were the
  build-entrypoint population. `fs-host` now gives the rest somewhere to go.
- **`future` (36 sites)** -- adding it to `async` would contradict that lib the
  same way `slurp` would contradict `fs`. Not a lib decision.
- **31 files declaring \"keep in sync\"** with no two identical, naming four
  different upstreams. Now scanned (ad22b32); deliberately NOT deduplicated,
  because CLAUDE.md requires an ADR first.
- **amu's 1,094-test suite is still ungated.** Its four fleet gates are all
  `:nbb-script`; none runs `clojure -M:test`. The claim surface went from 3 kits
  to 8 today, so this gap widened.

## Resume point

    nbb --classpath \".:scripts/nbb_compat\" scripts/verify-jvm-dependency-surface.cljs

reads 233 for `jvm-source`. That is the number to work against. The first
concrete step is a `:jvm-test` gate for amu, because everything landed in it
today is currently checked only when a person runs it."}]
